Quick Review Questions

       1. What is a class?
       2. What is an object?
       3. What keyword is commonly used to create an object?
       4. What represents the state of an object?
       5. What represents the behavior of an object?
       6. Can one class create multiple objects?
       7. Do different objects have to contain the same values?

                  Summary

The fundamental relationship to remember is:
                 CLASS
           (Blueprint/Template)
                    |
        -------------------------
        |           |           |
      Object      Object      Object
       #1          #2          #3
        |           |           |
    own state   own state   own state

In Java:

       class Student {
         String name;

         void display() {
           System.out.println(name);
            }
         }

Create objects:

      Student s1 = new Student();
      Student s2 = new Student();
        Initialize them:
     s1.name = "Juan";
     s2.name = "Maria";
     Use their behavior:
       s1.display();
       s2.display();

Java Constructors

A. Introduction to Java Constructors

A constructor is a special member of a class that is automatically executed when an object is created.

The primary purpose of a constructor is to initialize the object's state.

Consider the following Student class:

   public class Student {
       String name;
       int age;
   }

When an object is created, its instance variables initially contain Java's default values.

   Student student = new Student();

The initial values are:

    •  name = null
    •  age = 0

We can manually assign values, but a constructor lets us initialize the object immediately.

   public class Student {
       String name;
       int age;

       Student(String name, int age) {
           this.name = name;
           this.age = age;
       }
   }

   Student student = new Student("Juan", 20);

The general syntax of a constructor is:

   ClassName(parameters) {
       // initialization statements
   }

B. Rules for Creating a Java Constructor

   Rule 1: The constructor name must be the same as the class name.

        Correct:

          class Student {

              Student() {
                  System.out.println("Student created");
              }
          }

        Incorrect:

          class Student {

              student() {
                  // Incorrect constructor name
              }
          }

   Rule 2: A constructor does not have a return type.

        Correct:

          class Student {

              Student() {
                  System.out.println("Constructor executed");
              }
          }

        Incorrect:

          class Student {

              void Student() {
                  System.out.println("This is actually a method");
              }
          }

      The second example is not a constructor. Because it has void, Java treats it as a method named
       Student.

   Rule 3: Constructors are automatically called when an object is created.

       class Student {

           Student() {
               System.out.println("Constructor executed!");
           }
       }

public class Main {
           public static void main(String[] args) {
               Student s1 = new Student();
           }
       }

       Output:

          Constructor executed!

   Rule 4: Constructors are not inherited.

       class Person {

           Person() {
               System.out.println("Person constructor");
           }
       }

       class Student extends Person {
       }

      The constructor of Person is not inherited by Student. However, when a Student object is created,
      Java may invoke the superclass constructor through super().

   Rule 5: A class can have multiple constructors.

       class Student {

           Student() {
           }

           Student(String name) {
           }

           Student(String name, int age) {
           }
       }

       This is called constructor overloading.

   Rule 6: If no constructor is declared, Java may provide a default constructor.

       class Student {

           String name;
           int age;
       }

      The compiler effectively provides a no-argument constructor, allowing:

       Student s = new Student();

However, if a constructor is explicitly declared, Java no longer supplies the compiler-generated no-
      argument constructor automatically.

       class Student {

           String name;

           Student(String name) {
               this.name = name;
           }
       }

       This will cause an error:

          Student s = new Student();

      The correct form is:

          Student s = new Student("Juan");

C. Types of Java Constructors
For introductory Java, constructors can be discussed in three useful categories:

    •   Default Constructor
    •  No-Argument Constructor
    •  Parameterized Constructor

1. Default Constructor
   A default constructor is the constructor automatically provided by the Java compiler when no
    constructor is declared.

       class Student {

           String name;
           int age;
       }

    Conceptually, Java provides:

       Student() {
       }

       Student student = new Student();

   The fields receive Java's default values: name → null and age → 0.

2. No-Argument Constructor
   A no-argument constructor is a constructor explicitly written by the programmer that has no
    parameters.

       class Student {

           String name;
           int age;

           Student() {
               name = "Unknown";
               age = 0;
           }
       }

       Student student = new Student();

       System.out.println(student.name);
       System.out.println(student.age);

    Output:

       Unknown
       0

Default Constructor vs. No-Argument Constructor

               Default Constructor                      No-Argument Constructor
 Usually refers to compiler-provided constructor        Explicitly written by programmer
 Automatically generated if no constructor exists      Written manually
 Usually has no parameters                    Has no parameters
 Performs default initialization                  Can perform custom initialization

3. Parameterized Constructor
   A parameterized constructor accepts values when an object is created.

       class Student {

           String name;
           int age;

           Student(String name, int age) {
               this.name = name;
               this.age = age;
           }
       }

       Student student = new Student("Maria", 19);

       System.out.println(student.name);
       System.out.println(student.age);

    Output:

       Maria
       19

    Without a constructor, the values would have to be assigned separately. With a parameterized
    constructor, object creation and initialization can happen in one statement.

D. Understanding the this Keyword in Constructors
   The this keyword refers to the current object.

       class Student {

           String name;
           int age;

           Student(String name, int age) {
               this.name = name;
               this.age = age;
           }
       }

    In this.name = name, the left side refers to the instance variable and the right side refers to the
    constructor parameter.

    Therefore, this.name = name means: current object's name = parameter name.

E. Constructor Overloading
Constructor overloading occurs when a class has multiple constructors with different parameter lists.

   class Student {

       String name;
       int age;
       String course;

       Student() {
           name = "Unknown";
           age = 0;
           course = "Unknown";
       }

       Student(String name) {
           this.name = name;
           age = 0;
           course = "Unknown";
       }

       Student(String name, int age) {
           this.name = name;
           this.age = age;
           course = "Unknown";
       }

       Student(String name, int age, String course) {
           this.name = name;
           this.age = age;
           this.course = course;
       }
   }

Objects can now be created in different ways:

   Student s1 = new Student();
   Student s2 = new Student("Juan");
   Student s3 = new Student("Maria", 20);
   Student s4 = new Student("Pedro", 21, "BSIT");

Java determines which constructor to execute based on the arguments supplied.

Constructor Overloading Rules
The constructors must have different parameter lists.

Valid:

   Student()
   Student(String name)
   Student(String name, int age)

Also valid:

F. Constructor Chaining
When several constructors perform similar initialization, one constructor can call another constructor using
this(...).

   class Student {

       String name;
       int age;
       String course;

       Student() {
           this("Unknown", 0, "Unknown");
       }

       Student(String name) {
           this(name, 0, "Unknown");
       }

       Student(String name, int age) {
           this(name, age, "Unknown");
       }

       Student(String name, int age, String course) {
           this.name = name;
           this.age = age;
           this.course = course;
       }
   }

For example, new Student() calls the no-argument constructor, which then calls the three-parameter
constructor.

Important rule: a call to this(...) must be the first statement inside a constructor.

Correct:

   Student() {
       this("Unknown");
   }

Incorrect:

   Student() {
       System.out.println("Creating student");
       this("Unknown"); // Error
   }

G. Java Copy Constructor
Java does not have a special built-in copy constructor feature like C++, but a programmer can create a
constructor that receives an object of the same class and copies its values. This is commonly called a copy
constructor.

   class Student {

       String name;
       int age;
       String course;

       Student(String name, int age, String course) {
           this.name = name;
           this.age = age;
           this.course = course;
       }

       Student(Student other) {
           this.name = other.name;
           this.age = other.age;
           this.course = other.course;
       }
   }

   Student original =
       new Student("Juan", 20, "BSIT");

   Student copy =
       new Student(original);

The copy contains the same field values:

   Original                     Copy
   ----------------------       ----------------------
   name   = Juan                name   = Juan
   age    = 20                  age    = 20
   course = BSIT                course = BSIT

Copy Constructor vs. Assignment
Students often confuse object assignment with copying.

Assignment:

   Student copy = original;

Both variables refer to the same object.

Copy constructor:

   Student copy = new Student(original);

This creates a new Student object containing copied values. This distinction becomes important when
studying object references.

H. Constructor vs. Method

   class Student {

       String name;

       Student(String name) {
           this.name = name;
       }

       void displayName() {
           System.out.println(name);
       }
   }

Student(String name) is a constructor, while displayName() is a method.

                 Constructor                                Method
 Initializes an object                              Performs an operation
 Must have the same name as the class          Can have any valid name
 Cannot have a return type                   May have a return type
 Automatically invoked during object creation         Usually explicitly invoked
 Cannot be inherited                           Methods can be inherited
 Cannot be overridden                          Methods can be overridden
 Establishes initial object state                      Manipulates or accesses object behavior

Example:

   class Student {

       String name;
       int age;

       // Constructor
       Student(String name, int age) {
           this.name = name;
           this.age = age;
       }

       // Method
       void introduce() {
           System.out.println("My name is " + name);
           System.out.println("I am " + age + " years old.");
       }
   }

   Student student = new Student("Maria", 20);

   student.introduce();

The constructor is used during object creation and initialization, while the method is called afterward to
perform an operation.

I. Complete Example
The following program combines no-argument, parameterized, copy constructor, constructor overloading,
and methods.

   class Student {

       private String name;
       private int age;
       private String course;

       // No-argument constructor
       Student() {
           this("Unknown", 0, "Unknown");
       }

       // Parameterized constructor
       Student(String name, int age, String course) {
           this.name = name;
           this.age = age;
           this.course = course;
       }

       // Copy constructor
       Student(Student other) {
           this.name = other.name;
           this.age = other.age;
           this.course = other.course;
       }

       // Method
       void displayInformation() {
           System.out.println("Name: " + name);
           System.out.println("Age: " + age);
           System.out.println("Course: " + course);
           System.out.println();
       }
   }

   public class Main {

       public static void main(String[] args) {

           // No-argument constructor
           Student student1 = new Student();

           // Parameterized constructor
           Student student2 =
               new Student("Juan", 20, "BSIT");

           // Copy constructor
           Student student3 =
               new Student(student2);

           student1.displayInformation();
           student2.displayInformation();
           student3.displayInformation();

Static and This Keyword in Java

A. Java static Keyword
      The static keyword in Java is used to declare members that belong to the class itself rather than to
        individual objects. Normally, when a class is instantiated, each object receives its own copy of
       instance variables and methods. When a member is declared static, there is generally one class-
        level member shared by all objects of that class.

      General Syntax
            static dataType variableName;

            static returnType methodName() {
                // statements
            }

      Example
            class Student {
                String name;
                static String school = "Cavite State University";
            }

       Here, name is an instance variable while school is a static variable. Each Student object has its
      own name, while all Student objects share the same school.

       Creating Objects
            public class Main {
                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    Student s1 = new Student();
                    Student s2 = new Student();

                    s1.name = "Juan";
                    s2.name = "Maria";

                    System.out.println(s1.name);
                    System.out.println(s2.name);

                    System.out.println(Student.school);
                }
            }

            Output:

            Juan
            Maria
            Cavite State University

B. Java Static Variable
     A static variable is a variable that belongs to the class rather than to a particular object. It is also
      commonly called a class variable or static field. A static variable is useful when a value should be
       shared among all objects.

      Example 1: Shared School Name
            class Student {
                String name;
                static String school = "CvSU";
            }

            public class Main {
                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    Student s1 = new Student();
                    Student s2 = new Student();
                    Student s3 = new Student();

                    s1.name = "Juan";
                    s2.name = "Maria";
                    s3.name = "Pedro";

                    System.out.println(s1.name + " - " + Student.school);
                    System.out.println(s2.name + " - " + Student.school);
                    System.out.println(s3.name + " - " + Student.school);
                }
            }

            Output:
            Juan - CvSU
            Maria - CvSU
            Pedro - CvSU

      Example 2: Counting Objects
            class Student {

                String name;
                static int studentCount = 0;

                Student(String name) {
                    this.name = name;
                    studentCount++;
                }
            }

            public class Main {
                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    Student s1 = new Student("Juan");
                    Student s2 = new Student("Maria");
                    Student s3 = new Student("Pedro");

                    System.out.println("Number of students: "
                                       + Student.studentCount);

}
            }

            Output:
            Number of students: 3

       There is only one shared copy of studentCount. Every time a Student object is created,
       studentCount is incremented.

       Instance Variable vs Static Variable
        Instance Variable                           Static Variable
        Belongs to an object                   Belongs to a class
       Each object has its own copy           Shared by all objects
        Created for each object              One shared class-level copy
       Accessed through an object              Preferably accessed through class name
        Example: name                       Example: studentCount

C. Java Static Method
     A static method is a method that belongs to the class rather than an individual object. It can be
        called without creating an object.

      Syntax
            static returnType methodName() {
                // statements
            }

      Example
            class Calculator {

                static int add(int a, int b) {
                    return a + b;
                }
            }

            public class Main {

                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    int result = Calculator.add(10, 20);

                    System.out.println("Result: " + result);
                }
            }

            Output:
            Result: 30

      Another Example
            class MathUtility {

                static int square(int number) {
                    return number * number;
                }

static int cube(int number) {
                    return number * number * number;
                }
            }

            public class Main {
                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    System.out.println(MathUtility.square(5));
                    System.out.println(MathUtility.cube(3));
                }
            }

            Output:
            25
            27

     Why is main() Static?
      The standard Java entry point is public static void main(String[] args). The main method is static so
        that the JVM can execute it without first creating an object of the class.

            public class Main {

                public static void main(String[] args) {
                    System.out.println("Program started.");
                }
            }

D. Restrictions for Static Methods

    Restriction 1: A static method cannot directly access an instance variable
         class Student {

             String name;

             static void displayName() {
                 System.out.println(name); // ERROR
             }
         }

    The static method does not know which object's name should be used.

    Correct Version
         class Student {

             String name;

             static void displayName(Student student) {
                 System.out.println(student.name);
             }
         }

public class Main {

             public static void main(String[] args) {

                 Student s = new Student();
                 s.name = "Juan";

                 Student.displayName(s);
             }
         }

       Output:
         Juan

    Restriction 2: A static method cannot directly access an instance method
         class Student {

             void display() {
                 System.out.println("Student");
             }

             static void show() {
                 display(); // ERROR
             }
         }

    Correct approach: create or receive an object and call the instance method through that object.

         class Student {

             void display() {
                 System.out.println("Student");
             }

             static void show() {
                 Student s = new Student();
                 s.display();
             }
         }

    Restriction 3: this cannot be used in a static context
         class Student {

             String name;

             static void display() {
                 System.out.println(this.name); // ERROR
             }
         }

    The this keyword refers to the current object. A static method belongs to the class and does not have
    a current object.

What Can a Static Method Access?
    •   Static variables
    •  Other static methods

         class Example {

             static int number = 10;

             static void method1() {
                 System.out.println("Method 1");
             }

             static void method2() {
                 System.out.println(number);
                 method1();
             }
         }

E. Java Static Block
     A static block is a block of code declared using the static keyword. It is executed when the class is
         initialized by the JVM, before normal use of the class proceeds.

      Syntax
            static {
                // statements
            }

      Example
            class Student {

                static String school;

                static {
                    school = "Cavite State University";
                    System.out.println("Static block executed.");
                }
            }

            public class Main {

                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    System.out.println("Main method started.");

                    System.out.println(Student.school);
                }
            }

            Output:

            Static block executed.
            Main method started.
            Cavite State University

Static Block for Initialization
            class University {

                static String name;
                static int establishedYear;

                static {
                    name = "Cavite State University";
                    establishedYear = 1906;
                }
            }

            public class Main {

                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    System.out.println(University.name);
                    System.out.println(University.establishedYear);
                }
            }

            Output:

            Cavite State University
            1906

       Multiple Static Blocks
            class Example {

                static {
                    System.out.println("Static Block 1");
                }

                static {
                    System.out.println("Static Block 2");
                }

                static {
                    System.out.println("Static Block 3");
                }
            }

      They execute from top to bottom when the class is initialized.

            Static Block 1
            Static Block 2
            Static Block 3

F. Java this Keyword
    The this keyword refers to the current object. If s1.display() is called, then inside display(), this refers
     to s1.

         class Student {

String name;

             void display() {
                 System.out.println(this.name);
             }
         }

         public class Main {

             public static void main(String[] args) {

                 Student student = new Student();

                 student.name = "Juan";
                 student.display();
             }
         }

       Output:

         Juan

G. Different Ways to Use the this Keyword

    1. this to Refer to the Current Object
         class Student {

             String name;

             void display() {
                 System.out.println(this.name);
             }
         }

    2. this to Resolve Variable Shadowing
   One of the most common uses of this is distinguishing an instance variable from a parameter having
    the same name.

         class Student {

             String name;
             int age;

             Student(String name, int age) {
                 this.name = name;
                 this.age = age;
             }

             void display() {
                 System.out.println("Name: " + this.name);
                 System.out.println("Age: " + this.age);
             }
         }

public class Main {

             public static void main(String[] args) {

                 Student s1 = new Student("Juan", 20);
                 Student s2 = new Student("Maria", 21);

                 s1.display();
                 s2.display();
             }
         }

       Output:

         Name: Juan
         Age: 20
         Name: Maria
         Age: 21

    3. this to Call the Current Class Method
         class Student {

             void study() {
                 System.out.println("Studying Java...");
             }

             void start() {
                 System.out.println("Student started.");
                 this.study();
             }
         }

         public class Main {

             public static void main(String[] args) {

                 Student student = new Student();
                 student.start();
             }
         }

       Output:

         Student started.
         Studying Java...

    4. this to Call Another Constructor
    The this() syntax can call another constructor within the same class. This is called constructor
     chaining.

         class Student {

             String name;
             int age;

             Student() {

this("Unknown", 0);
             }

             Student(String name, int age) {
                 this.name = name;
                 this.age = age;
             }

             void display() {
                 System.out.println("Name: " + name);
                 System.out.println("Age: " + age);
             }
         }

         public class Main {

             public static void main(String[] args) {

                 Student s1 = new Student();
                 Student s2 = new Student("Juan", 20);

                 s1.display();
                 s2.display();
             }
         }

       Output:

         Name: Unknown
         Age: 0
         Name: Juan
         Age: 20

    Important rule: When this() is used to call another constructor, it must be the first statement in the
     constructor.

    5. this to Pass the Current Object as an Argument
         class Student {

             String name;

             Student(String name) {
                 this.name = name;
             }

             void register(Student student) {
                 System.out.println(student.name + " is registered.");
             }

             void enroll() {
                 register(this);
             }
         }

         public class Main {

public static void main(String[] args) {

                 Student student = new Student("Juan");
                 student.enroll();
             }
         }

       Output:

         Juan is registered.

    Here, register(this) passes the current Student object as an argument.

    6. this to Return the Current Object
   A method can return the current object using this. This is commonly used for method chaining.

         class Student {

             String name;
             int age;

             Student setName(String name) {
                 this.name = name;
                 return this;
             }

             Student setAge(int age) {
                 this.age = age;
                 return this;
             }

             void display() {
                 System.out.println(name);
                 System.out.println(age);
             }
         }

         public class Main {

             public static void main(String[] args) {

                 Student student = new Student();

                 student.setName("Juan")
                        .setAge(20)
                        .display();
             }
         }

       Output:

         Juan
         20

